1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,368
A fighter jet lands at around two

2
00:00:02,368 --> 00:00:05,096
hundred and forty kilometres per hour.

3
00:00:05,276 --> 00:00:06,782
On a normal airport,

4
00:00:06,782 --> 00:00:08,966
it needs a runway more than a

5
00:00:08,966 --> 00:00:10,698
kilometre long to stop.

6
00:00:10,878 --> 00:00:12,441
On an aircraft carrier,

7
00:00:12,441 --> 00:00:14,617
it has about one hundred metres.

8
00:00:14,797 --> 00:00:16,621
So the ship catches it,

9
00:00:16,621 --> 00:00:18,128
with a steel cable,

10
00:00:18,128 --> 00:00:20,903
and stops it in around two seconds.

11
00:00:21,083 --> 00:00:24,588
Today, ten parts of an aircraft carrier,

12
00:00:24,588 --> 00:00:26,691
and why each one exists.

13
00:00:27,040 --> 00:00:30,465
An aircraft carrier is a floating airport,

14
00:00:30,465 --> 00:00:33,726
squeezed onto a deck about three hundred

15
00:00:33,726 --> 00:00:35,357
metres long, moving,

16
00:00:35,357 --> 00:00:37,965
rolling and pitching in the sea.

17
00:00:38,145 --> 00:00:41,192
Almost every part of it exists because

18
00:00:41,192 --> 00:00:43,277
there is not enough space.

19
00:00:43,457 --> 00:00:46,606
Planes must launch without a long runway,

20
00:00:46,606 --> 00:00:48,680
land without a long runway,

21
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:51,061
and be parked where there is no

22
00:00:51,061 --> 00:00:51,445
room.

23
00:00:51,795 --> 00:00:54,825
As always, every fact here comes from

24
00:00:54,825 --> 00:00:56,053
a named source,

25
00:00:56,053 --> 00:00:58,347
navies and naval references,

26
00:00:58,347 --> 00:01:00,804
all listed in the description.

27
00:01:00,984 --> 00:01:02,830
Let's start with the cables.

28
00:01:03,180 --> 00:01:05,955
Why are there steel cables stretched across

29
00:01:05,955 --> 00:01:08,021
the deck of an aircraft carrier?

30
00:01:08,201 --> 00:01:10,035
They are arresting wires.

31
00:01:10,215 --> 00:01:12,797
A landing jet catches one of them

32
00:01:12,797 --> 00:01:13,736
with a hook,

33
00:01:13,736 --> 00:01:15,614
and the cable pulls out,

34
00:01:15,614 --> 00:01:17,413
slowing the plane down.

35
00:01:17,593 --> 00:01:20,648
Below deck, each wire is connected to

36
00:01:20,648 --> 00:01:23,867
a large machine that absorbs the energy

37
00:01:23,867 --> 00:01:25,105
of the landing.

38
00:01:25,455 --> 00:01:27,602
The machine is the clever part.

39
00:01:27,782 --> 00:01:30,375
It controls how hard the cable pulls,

40
00:01:30,375 --> 00:01:32,897
depending on the weight and speed of

41
00:01:32,897 --> 00:01:33,878
each aircraft.

42
00:01:34,058 --> 00:01:36,082
Too soft, and the plane rolls off

43
00:01:36,082 --> 00:01:37,308
the end of the deck.

44
00:01:37,488 --> 00:01:39,674
Too hard, and the hook or the

45
00:01:39,674 --> 00:01:41,633
plane could be torn apart.

46
00:01:41,813 --> 00:01:44,349
The settings are changed for every single

47
00:01:44,349 --> 00:01:44,843
landing.

48
00:01:45,194 --> 00:01:49,094
American carriers have three or four wires.

49
00:01:49,274 --> 00:01:51,506
On carriers with four wires,

50
00:01:51,506 --> 00:01:53,498
pilots aim for the third,

51
00:01:53,498 --> 00:01:56,287
because it gives the safest margin.

52
00:01:56,467 --> 00:01:58,376
Every landing is graded,

53
00:01:58,376 --> 00:02:01,001
and pilots are ranked by how well

54
00:02:01,001 --> 00:02:01,796
they land.

55
00:02:01,976 --> 00:02:04,557
Stopping a jet in about two seconds

56
00:02:04,557 --> 00:02:07,359
pushes the pilot hard into the straps,

57
00:02:07,359 --> 00:02:09,940
and most say it never feels normal.

58
00:02:10,291 --> 00:02:11,954
Why do navy jets have a hook

59
00:02:11,954 --> 00:02:12,845
under the tail?

60
00:02:13,025 --> 00:02:14,329
It is the tailhook,

61
00:02:14,329 --> 00:02:17,007
the other half of the arresting system.

62
00:02:17,187 --> 00:02:20,322
Before landing, the pilot lowers the hook.

63
00:02:20,502 --> 00:02:22,999
It hangs behind the plane and drags

64
00:02:22,999 --> 00:02:24,070
along the deck,

65
00:02:24,070 --> 00:02:26,353
ready to snag one of the cables.

66
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:29,162
Air force versions of the same jets

67
00:02:29,162 --> 00:02:31,490
often have a much smaller hook,

68
00:02:31,490 --> 00:02:32,617
or none at all.

69
00:02:32,967 --> 00:02:35,663
The hook has to take enormous forces

70
00:02:35,663 --> 00:02:37,611
in a fraction of a second.

71
00:02:37,791 --> 00:02:40,038
So it is built into a strong

72
00:02:40,038 --> 00:02:42,447
part of the plane's structure,

73
00:02:42,447 --> 00:02:45,497
and navy jets have stronger frames and

74
00:02:45,497 --> 00:02:47,183
landing gear overall.

75
00:02:47,363 --> 00:02:49,659
That extra strength adds weight,

76
00:02:49,659 --> 00:02:52,529
which is one reason carrier aircraft are

77
00:02:52,529 --> 00:02:55,327
heavier than their land based versions.

78
00:02:55,678 --> 00:02:58,580
Navy pilots do not flare and float

79
00:02:58,580 --> 00:03:01,483
onto the deck like airline pilots.

80
00:03:01,663 --> 00:03:04,190
They fly straight into the deck at

81
00:03:04,190 --> 00:03:05,305
a steady angle,

82
00:03:05,305 --> 00:03:08,129
in what looks like a controlled crash.

83
00:03:08,309 --> 00:03:11,165
The landing gear is built to absorb

84
00:03:11,165 --> 00:03:14,021
that impact, and the hook is placed

85
00:03:14,021 --> 00:03:16,305
so it is sure to drag across

86
00:03:16,305 --> 00:03:17,121
the wires.

87
00:03:17,472 --> 00:03:19,817
Why do navy pilots push the throttle

88
00:03:19,817 --> 00:03:22,098
to full power the moment they touch

89
00:03:22,098 --> 00:03:22,684
the deck?

90
00:03:22,864 --> 00:03:25,291
In case the hook misses the wires.

91
00:03:25,471 --> 00:03:28,021
If the hook bounces over every cable,

92
00:03:28,021 --> 00:03:30,227
the plane is running out of deck

93
00:03:30,227 --> 00:03:30,916
very fast.

94
00:03:31,096 --> 00:03:33,931
With the engines already at full power,

95
00:03:33,931 --> 00:03:36,402
it can take off again immediately.

96
00:03:36,752 --> 00:03:39,538
A missed landing like this is called

97
00:03:39,538 --> 00:03:40,235
a bolter.

98
00:03:40,415 --> 00:03:42,762
The jet roars off the end of

99
00:03:42,762 --> 00:03:44,103
the angled deck,

100
00:03:44,103 --> 00:03:46,870
climbs, circles, and tries again.

101
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:48,931
If the engines were idle,

102
00:03:48,931 --> 00:03:51,941
they would need several seconds to spool

103
00:03:51,941 --> 00:03:54,424
up, and the plane would fall into

104
00:03:54,424 --> 00:03:55,026
the sea.

105
00:03:55,206 --> 00:03:58,491
Full power on landing turns a disaster

106
00:03:58,491 --> 00:04:00,047
into a second try.

107
00:04:00,397 --> 00:04:02,391
If the hook does catch a wire,

108
00:04:02,391 --> 00:04:04,651
the cable is strong enough to hold

109
00:04:04,651 --> 00:04:06,445
the jet even at full power.

110
00:04:06,625 --> 00:04:08,951
Only when the plane has stopped does

111
00:04:08,951 --> 00:04:11,084
the pilot pull the throttle back.

112
00:04:11,264 --> 00:04:13,890
It is one of the strangest rules

113
00:04:13,890 --> 00:04:14,874
in aviation.

114
00:04:15,054 --> 00:04:16,161
To land safely,

115
00:04:16,161 --> 00:04:18,375
you must be ready to take off.

116
00:04:18,725 --> 00:04:20,901
Why does the deck of an aircraft

117
00:04:20,901 --> 00:04:23,281
carrier have a long slot running to

118
00:04:23,281 --> 00:04:23,961
the front?

119
00:04:24,141 --> 00:04:25,615
It is the catapult.

120
00:04:25,795 --> 00:04:28,409
A heavy jet cannot reach take off

121
00:04:28,409 --> 00:04:31,182
speed in less than a hundred metres

122
00:04:31,182 --> 00:04:32,053
on its own.

123
00:04:32,233 --> 00:04:35,285
The catapult grabs the nose wheel and

124
00:04:35,285 --> 00:04:37,347
throws the plane forward,

125
00:04:37,347 --> 00:04:40,234
from zero to around two hundred and

126
00:04:40,234 --> 00:04:43,369
sixty kilometres per hour in about two

127
00:04:43,369 --> 00:04:44,029
seconds.

128
00:04:44,379 --> 00:04:48,119
For decades, catapults were powered by steam

129
00:04:48,119 --> 00:04:51,180
from the ship's boilers or reactors,

130
00:04:51,180 --> 00:04:54,751
pushing pistons along a cylinder under the

131
00:04:54,751 --> 00:04:55,176
deck.

132
00:04:55,356 --> 00:04:58,688
The steam catapult was a British invention

133
00:04:58,688 --> 00:05:00,592
of the nineteen fifties.

134
00:05:00,772 --> 00:05:02,821
You can still see the clouds of

135
00:05:02,821 --> 00:05:05,267
steam rising from the slot after each

136
00:05:05,267 --> 00:05:05,729
launch.

137
00:05:06,080 --> 00:05:08,595
The newest American carrier,

138
00:05:08,595 --> 00:05:10,211
the Gerald R Ford,

139
00:05:10,211 --> 00:05:13,894
uses an electromagnetic catapult instead,

140
00:05:13,894 --> 00:05:17,397
a linear electric motor that pushes the

141
00:05:17,397 --> 00:05:19,733
plane with magnetic force.

142
00:05:19,913 --> 00:05:22,686
It can be adjusted more precisely for

143
00:05:22,686 --> 00:05:24,559
light and heavy aircraft,

144
00:05:24,559 --> 00:05:26,807
and it puts less stress on the

145
00:05:26,807 --> 00:05:27,332
planes.

146
00:05:27,512 --> 00:05:28,812
The idea is old,

147
00:05:28,812 --> 00:05:31,981
but the power behind it keeps changing.

148
00:05:32,332 --> 00:05:34,491
Why does a metal wall rise from

149
00:05:34,491 --> 00:05:36,650
the deck behind a jet before it

150
00:05:36,650 --> 00:05:37,277
launches?

151
00:05:37,457 --> 00:05:39,710
It is the jet blast deflector.

152
00:05:39,890 --> 00:05:42,327
When the jet goes to full power

153
00:05:42,327 --> 00:05:43,585
on the catapult,

154
00:05:43,585 --> 00:05:47,123
its engines blast hot gas straight backwards.

155
00:05:47,303 --> 00:05:48,459
Without the wall,

156
00:05:48,459 --> 00:05:50,839
that blast would hit the planes and

157
00:05:50,839 --> 00:05:52,539
people waiting behind it.

158
00:05:52,889 --> 00:05:55,354
The deflector tilts up from the deck

159
00:05:55,354 --> 00:05:57,683
and sends the hot exhaust upwards,

160
00:05:57,683 --> 00:05:58,984
away from the crew.

161
00:05:59,164 --> 00:06:01,226
Because the blast is so hot,

162
00:06:01,226 --> 00:06:03,730
the panels are cooled by sea water

163
00:06:03,730 --> 00:06:06,234
running through pipes inside them.

164
00:06:06,414 --> 00:06:09,420
Without cooling, they would warp after a

165
00:06:09,420 --> 00:06:10,397
few launches.

166
00:06:10,747 --> 00:06:13,955
The deflector lets the carrier launch planes

167
00:06:13,955 --> 00:06:15,267
one after another,

168
00:06:15,267 --> 00:06:18,038
with the next jet waiting just behind.

169
00:06:18,218 --> 00:06:20,012
On a busy flight deck,

170
00:06:20,012 --> 00:06:21,561
every metre counts.

171
00:06:21,741 --> 00:06:24,092
A simple wall makes it possible to

172
00:06:24,092 --> 00:06:26,513
line up aircraft behind each other,

173
00:06:26,513 --> 00:06:29,279
instead of spreading them out across the

174
00:06:29,279 --> 00:06:29,624
deck.

175
00:06:29,975 --> 00:06:32,424
Why is the landing area of an

176
00:06:32,424 --> 00:06:35,379
aircraft carrier set at an angle to

177
00:06:35,379 --> 00:06:36,139
the ship?

178
00:06:36,319 --> 00:06:37,555
On early carriers,

179
00:06:37,555 --> 00:06:40,369
planes landed straight down the length of

180
00:06:40,369 --> 00:06:40,987
the deck.

181
00:06:41,167 --> 00:06:43,091
If a plane missed the wires,

182
00:06:43,091 --> 00:06:45,565
it crashed into the planes parked at

183
00:06:45,565 --> 00:06:46,252
the front.

184
00:06:46,432 --> 00:06:48,815
Barriers were used to stop them,

185
00:06:48,815 --> 00:06:51,273
and many aircraft were destroyed.

186
00:06:51,624 --> 00:06:53,942
In the early nineteen fifties,

187
00:06:53,942 --> 00:06:56,880
British officers came up with a simple

188
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:57,266
idea.

189
00:06:57,446 --> 00:06:59,974
Angle the landing area to the left,

190
00:06:59,974 --> 00:07:01,997
so it points off the side of

191
00:07:01,997 --> 00:07:02,647
the ship.

192
00:07:02,827 --> 00:07:05,311
A plane that misses the wires simply

193
00:07:05,311 --> 00:07:07,450
flies off the end of the angled

194
00:07:07,450 --> 00:07:08,899
deck and goes around.

195
00:07:09,079 --> 00:07:11,572
The front of the ship stays clear

196
00:07:11,572 --> 00:07:14,141
for parking and catapult launches.

197
00:07:14,492 --> 00:07:17,510
The angled deck changed carrier design forever.

198
00:07:17,690 --> 00:07:20,526
It allowed landings and launches at the

199
00:07:20,526 --> 00:07:22,926
same time, and made bolters safe.

200
00:07:23,106 --> 00:07:26,244
Almost every large carrier built since has

201
00:07:26,244 --> 00:07:26,543
one.

202
00:07:26,723 --> 00:07:29,243
A few degrees of paint on the

203
00:07:29,243 --> 00:07:32,545
deck turned the most dangerous part of

204
00:07:32,545 --> 00:07:35,152
naval aviation into a routine.

205
00:07:35,502 --> 00:07:38,475
Why do carrier pilots follow a ball

206
00:07:38,475 --> 00:07:39,240
of light?

207
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:41,559
On the side of the deck there

208
00:07:41,559 --> 00:07:43,182
is a system of lights,

209
00:07:43,182 --> 00:07:45,689
called the optical landing system.

210
00:07:45,869 --> 00:07:49,231
Pilots call the central light the meatball,

211
00:07:49,231 --> 00:07:50,560
or just the ball.

212
00:07:50,740 --> 00:07:53,796
Its position, compared with a row of

213
00:07:53,796 --> 00:07:55,748
green reference lights,

214
00:07:55,748 --> 00:07:58,805
tells the pilot whether the plane is

215
00:07:58,805 --> 00:08:01,436
too high, too low or exactly on

216
00:08:01,436 --> 00:08:02,710
the glide path.

217
00:08:03,060 --> 00:08:05,945
The system uses special lenses that only

218
00:08:05,945 --> 00:08:08,326
show the ball at the right height

219
00:08:08,326 --> 00:08:10,490
to a pilot at the right angle.

220
00:08:10,670 --> 00:08:12,702
If the plane is too high,

221
00:08:12,702 --> 00:08:15,952
the ball appears above the green lights.

222
00:08:16,132 --> 00:08:18,912
Too low, and it drops below them,

223
00:08:18,912 --> 00:08:21,775
and turns red if it is dangerously

224
00:08:21,775 --> 00:08:22,112
low.

225
00:08:22,292 --> 00:08:24,399
The pilot keeps the ball in the

226
00:08:24,399 --> 00:08:26,030
middle all the way down.

227
00:08:26,380 --> 00:08:29,079
The idea started with a British mirror

228
00:08:29,079 --> 00:08:31,636
landing aid in the nineteen fifties,

229
00:08:31,636 --> 00:08:33,555
using a mirror and a light.

230
00:08:33,735 --> 00:08:36,618
Next to it stands a landing signal

231
00:08:36,618 --> 00:08:40,605
officer, an experienced pilot who watches every

232
00:08:40,605 --> 00:08:43,489
approach and talks to the pilot by

233
00:08:43,489 --> 00:08:43,998
radio.

234
00:08:44,178 --> 00:08:47,143
Lights and a human eye work together

235
00:08:47,143 --> 00:08:48,543
on every landing.

236
00:08:48,893 --> 00:08:51,223
Why is the control tower of an

237
00:08:51,223 --> 00:08:54,564
aircraft carrier almost always on the right

238
00:08:54,564 --> 00:08:54,953
side?

239
00:08:55,133 --> 00:08:57,453
The tower is called the island,

240
00:08:57,453 --> 00:08:59,250
and it holds the bridge,

241
00:08:59,250 --> 00:09:01,495
the flight control and radars.

242
00:09:01,675 --> 00:09:03,424
Putting it on one edge leaves the

243
00:09:03,424 --> 00:09:04,589
rest of the deck free.

244
00:09:04,769 --> 00:09:06,991
The choice of the right side is

245
00:09:06,991 --> 00:09:09,642
partly about the angled landing deck,

246
00:09:09,642 --> 00:09:11,433
which points to the left.

247
00:09:11,784 --> 00:09:14,805
There is also a historical reason that

248
00:09:14,805 --> 00:09:15,998
is often given.

249
00:09:16,178 --> 00:09:18,921
Early propeller planes tended to pull to

250
00:09:18,921 --> 00:09:21,047
the left when they went to full

251
00:09:21,047 --> 00:09:23,790
power, and pilots usually turned left if

252
00:09:23,790 --> 00:09:25,710
they had to abort a landing.

253
00:09:25,890 --> 00:09:27,725
An island on the right was out

254
00:09:27,725 --> 00:09:28,398
of the way.

255
00:09:28,578 --> 00:09:30,821
Once that layout became standard,

256
00:09:30,821 --> 00:09:33,064
the rest of the deck was designed

257
00:09:33,064 --> 00:09:33,744
around it.

258
00:09:34,094 --> 00:09:36,552
A few carriers have been built with

259
00:09:36,552 --> 00:09:38,168
the island on the left,

260
00:09:38,168 --> 00:09:40,346
but they were rare and were not

261
00:09:40,346 --> 00:09:40,978
repeated.

262
00:09:41,158 --> 00:09:44,802
The British Queen Elizabeth class carriers have

263
00:09:44,802 --> 00:09:47,748
two islands, one for steering the ship

264
00:09:47,748 --> 00:09:51,004
and one for controlling flight operations.

265
00:09:51,184 --> 00:09:53,526
Even the tower of a carrier is

266
00:09:53,526 --> 00:09:55,166
a careful compromise.

267
00:09:55,516 --> 00:09:57,842
Why do the wings of navy aircraft

268
00:09:57,842 --> 00:09:58,406
fold up?

269
00:09:58,586 --> 00:10:00,026
To save space.

270
00:10:00,206 --> 00:10:02,914
A carrier may carry dozens of aircraft,

271
00:10:02,914 --> 00:10:04,788
and they all have to fit on

272
00:10:04,788 --> 00:10:07,079
the deck and in the hangar below.

273
00:10:07,259 --> 00:10:10,341
Folding the wings can reduce a plane's

274
00:10:10,341 --> 00:10:11,314
width a lot,

275
00:10:11,314 --> 00:10:13,746
so more planes fit in the same

276
00:10:13,746 --> 00:10:16,584
space, and they can be moved around

277
00:10:16,584 --> 00:10:17,557
more easily.

278
00:10:17,908 --> 00:10:19,623
The fold adds hinges,

279
00:10:19,623 --> 00:10:21,911
locks and hydraulic systems,

280
00:10:21,911 --> 00:10:24,525
which add weight and complexity.

281
00:10:24,705 --> 00:10:27,579
A wing that fails to lock properly

282
00:10:27,579 --> 00:10:30,621
before take off is a serious danger,

283
00:10:30,621 --> 00:10:33,494
so the system has multiple checks.

284
00:10:33,674 --> 00:10:36,365
Navy versions of aircraft like the F

285
00:10:36,365 --> 00:10:38,832
thirty-five C have folding wings,

